### fold-space-transport
- Fold space is finite. Estimates of the number of folds in nature run from 1,000 to 10,000 depending on the
  model, Chothia's early estimate near 1,000 (Grant, Lee and Orengo, *Genome Biology* 5:107, 2004);
  Gerstein's 1997 census treats it as a limited molecular parts list sufficient for every organism; and
  later work reports fold coverage plateauing (Roche and Brüls, *Sci. Rep.*, 2015).
- *Claude's gloss:* this is crystallography's situation one level up. The document starts the chemistry from
  crystallography because it never took the set quotient: the 230 space groups are a finite classification of
  structured-equivalence data and Wyckoff positions are orbits. Fold space is a finite set of topologies, each
  a class of chains under "has this arrangement of secondary-structure elements". Prediction from a template
  is then transport along an identification. A sequence alignment between a target and a template of known
  structure is a partial equivalence of residue positions — `AtomMap`'s shape one level up, a relabelling with
  its inverse carried as data — and the structure transports along it; `scope-respects` and
  `bind-transport` already state that a claim transports along identity and along nothing else. Where the
  alignment identifies, the structure transports; at a gap nothing transports and the fiber stays wide, which
  is correct: an unaligned loop has no warrant from the template, and a sound predictor says so rather than
  inventing coordinates. "Template-based modelling" becomes a transport whose domain is the aligned positions,
  with an output fiber exactly as narrow as the identification it travels along.
