  from the pieces of the electric operator). Its U(1) factor: tr(τ · 1) = i ≠ 0 for U(1), so the curvature is the
  Lie-algebra part of the holonomy, as tr(τ_I h) already is for SU(N), and two loops give
  S_α S_β = −¼(h_α h_β − h_α h_β⁻¹ − h_α⁻¹ h_β + h_α⁻¹ h_β⁻¹). Where the loops act: the triangulation is adapted to
  the graph, so a segment s_j holds no vertex of it and every new arc is attached nearer v than every arc already
  there. An edge's end at v is its stack of segment labels from v outward; a loop through it pushes a new innermost
  segment with the label shifted by ±1; the electric operator reads only the innermost labels; at an arc's ends the
  edge's two halves are collinear, every ε(I,J,K) is 0, the volume is 0, and neither operator acts. Proved
  (`loop-keeps-beyond`): the label of each edge beyond every arc, the flux it carries from one vertex to the next,
  is unchanged by every loop, and Ĥ_E is diagonal in the labels, so it is a constant of motion of Ĥ_E + Ĥ_B.
  Ĥ splits into sectors by these labels, and in each it is a sum of commuting operators, one per vertex, on that
  vertex's gravitational state, innermost labels and arcs. Three consequences.
    The magnetic term cannot superpose two paths of the flux between vertices, on any graph, so it cannot spread
  the flux: Coulomb's 1/r does not come from this regularization.
    The arcs carry flux at no cost. Symmetrized with its adjoint (which removes arcs), the operator below any state
  whose innermost labels are all 0 is a copy of the operator below the arc-free state with labels 0 (only the
  inner labels and the gravitational state enter the diagonal), and every innermost pattern with the vertex's
  charge is reached (a pair of loops moves two units at once, and one unit in through edge 0 and out through 1 is
  e_02 + e_21). So the infimum of each vertex's spectrum is the same for every flux pattern through it, and the
  lowest energy of a separated charge pair does not depend on the separation: no Coulomb force and no confinement.
    Unsymmetrized, as Thiemann defines it, the operator is not symmetric (QSD V §4.2, which gives a counterexample
  using Ĥ^B), and it only adds arcs, so it is triangular in the number of arc layers: every eigenvalue is an
  eigenvalue of its layer-preserving part (Ĥ_E and the ½ of each loop taken twice) on some graph, and the lowest
  energy is not a variational question for it.
  What the magnetic term needs, to give Coulomb's law, is loops that run between vertices along the graph without
  changing it: Sahlmann and Thiemann derive the Maxwell field propagating on a graph γ, with gravity in a
  semiclassical state (CQG 23 (2006) 867 and 909), and Giesel and Thiemann's algebraic quantum gravity keeps the
  graph fixed (CQG 24 (2007) 2465); how their magnetic terms are regularized is not yet read here. Where
  that Ĥ_B dominates (weak coupling, the Coulomb phase of compact U(1) in four spacetime dimensions: Guth, PRD 21
  (1980) 2291; Fröhlich and Spencer, CMP 83 (1982) 411), the static energy of charges is, at leading order, the
  minimum of the electric form over real flux obeying Gauss's law: electrostatics on the graph,
    E = ρᵀ (D W⁻¹ Dᵀ)⁻¹ ρ,   W = Σ_v w_v,
  D the incidence matrix and w_v the vertex forms derived above (positive, Thiemann's A†A form of Ĥ_E, QSD V
  eq. 4.6). At vertices without charge w_v lies in ℚ(3^{1/4}), the r(2,·), r(4,·) parts cancelling as above.
- Work state, next (2026-11-18): Coulomb's law from the quantum geometry. (i) A graph with room for the flux to
  spread: the diamond net, every vertex four-valent with tetrahedral edges, which fixes every ε; (ii) Ĥ_B with the
  graph's own loops (its six-edge rings), K_lp from `k-terms`; (iii) E(ρ) for a charge pair on finite pieces of
